    thinking: "**Analyzing Container Mechanics**\n\nI\'m currently focused on dissecting the interplay of containers and aspect ratios, specifically with the provided code snippet. The key takeaway is how children within the designated container are positioned, seemingly leveraging percentage-based units and container query units, `cqw`. I\'m trying to wrap my head around the specifics of this relationship and how it facilitates responsive design within the container\'s boundaries.\n\n\n**Deciphering Text Scaling**\n\nI\'ve made a crucial observation. With `aspect-[800/600]` and `container-type:inline-size`, `1cqw` precisely mirrors 1% of the HeroSection\'s width. The fixed 4:3 aspect ratio means `1cqw` relates proportionally to height too. Now, the provided text example uses `10.2cqw`, ensuring identical proportions across devices, which aligns perfectly with the goal of consistent text positioning for desktop, mobile, and tablets. This looks like a complete fix!\n\n\n**Investigating Line Breaks**\n\nI\'m now tracing the unexpected line break in \"POWER YOUR\". I am examining the `motion.h1` element\'s positioning and width specifications. It looks like the `className=\"absolute left-[0%] w-[100%]\"` might be the culprit, coupled with the block level styling of the first `<span>` and the `tracking-[-0.04em]` on the second. It\'s time to test if removing the `w-[100%]` forces the intended layout.\n\n\n**Examining Width Conflicts**\n\nI\'ve hit a potential snag. While exploring the line break issue, I noticed the inner `div`\'s width is set to \"61%\", yet `motion.h1` is set to `w-[100%]` of that parent. This conflict is the source of the issue. The absolute positioning combined with this discrepancy likely forces the content to wrap, leading to the unwanted line break. My next step will be to explore alternative width declarations for the `motion.h1` element to resolve this conflict.\n\n\n**Calculating Width Discrepancies**\n\nI\'ve made a breakthrough, and I now believe I know the root cause! The `motion.h1` element is assigned a width equal to 61% of its parent. However, the text size, defined as `10.2cqw`, translates to a width greater than 61cqw, due to text rendering in ems. This width discrepancy between text size and container width forces the text to wrap across two lines.\n\n\n**Calculating Text Overflow**\n\nI\'ve quantified why \"PONTETIALE\" is cut off. Its font size, `11.4cqw`, spans roughly 65.5cqw based on character widths. This, combined with the container\'s starting position at 37cqw, totals 102.5cqw. Since it exceeds 100cqw, this overruns the hero container and causes overflow. The `overflow-hidden` property further exacerbates this issue by clipping the excess text.\n\n\n**Verifying Button Placement**\n\nI\'m now shifting focus to the button\'s position within the layout. Specifically, I\'m examining lines 494-525 of the code. The `style` attribute sets `left` to 38.5% and `bottom` to 10.5%. The button\'s relative positioning with respect to the container is critical, so I will now verify these values and their impact on the overall visual harmony, relative to the typography. I also need to verify that it\'s correctly aligned to the typography and left of the `P` in `POWER`/`PONTETIAL`.\n\n\n\n**Verifying Text and Button Alignment**\n\nI\'m now focusing on optimizing the button placement and text alignment to meet all layout requirements. I am verifying the alignment of \"POWER YOUR\" on one line, with \"PONTETIALE\" fully visible, ensuring that the proportions and positioning of the button and text elements are perfectly consistent across various screen sizes (desktop, mobile, and tablets). Specifically, I\'m checking the calculated `left` position for the button.\n\n\n\n**Refining Element Positioning**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on fine-tuning the `motion.h1` element\'s properties. I\'ve confirmed that `whitespace-nowrap` on both text lines is essential for preventing unwanted line breaks. I\'m focusing on positioning and dimensions relative to the hero image, specifically lines 380-492. These lines should specify the absolute positioning of the text block relative to the hero image and the correct calculated button placement.\n\n\n**Investigating Font Sizing**\n\nI am now closely inspecting the font-sizing properties of the `motion.h1` element in the hero section. I\'ve re-examined the code snippet and noted that the parent `div` has a width of 61%, while the `motion.h1` is set to `w-[100%]`. This sets up a width discrepancy, so I will now carefully evaluate the font size declaration using `cqw` units, to see if that resolves the issue of text overflowing the container.\n\n\n"
