    thinking: "**Analyzing Motion\'s Position**\n\nI\'m now carefully examining the positioning of `motion.h1` within the codebase. Specifically, I\'m digging into how its initial state and subsequent animations affect the display of text, like \"POWER YOUR\" and \"POTENTIALE.\" I need to understand how the motion library influences the visual hierarchy and overall user experience in this context.\n\n\n**Aligning Visual Elements**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the alignment inconsistencies between `motion.h1` and the button section. The current positioning, derived from the parent `div`\'s dimensions and the `left-[0%]` style, seems counterintuitive. I\'ve calculated that `left: 0%` actually places the element at 37% from the left edge of the hero container. I\'m considering why we aren\'t directly aligning `motion.h1` with the button using `left: \"2.4%\"` of the width of its parent element or `left: \"1.5cqw\"`.\n\n\n**Investigating Text Widths**\n\nI\'m now diving into the precise calculations of the text widths within `motion.h1`. I\'ve realized that setting `w-[100%]` forces the heading\'s width to be 100% of its parent, which is 61% of the hero section. Replacing it with `w-max` or `w-auto`, along with `whitespace-nowrap`, would prevent text wrapping, a key point. I\'m checking font sizes and character counts. I\'m exploring how font size, tracking, and character length together determine how the text fits within its container.\n\n\n**Optimizing Horizontal Placement**\n\nI\'ve just finalized the horizontal placement calculations for the text. I\'ve pinpointed `38.5cqw` as a starting point, adding `52.5cqw` for the text width, which results in `91.0cqw`, ensuring the \"E\" in \"POTENTIALE\" is visible and leaving 9cqw of padding. Importantly, \"POWER YOUR\" remains on a single line. The beauty of this is consistent positioning across all screen sizes thanks to `cqw`, `inline-size`, and `aspect-[800/600]`.\n\n\n**Refining Typography Styles**\n\nI\'m now concentrating on adjusting the typography and text styling within the hero section. I\'ve been experimenting with the font sizes of \"POWER YOUR\" and \"PONTETIALE.\" I have found that decreasing the font size for \"POWER YOUR\" to `8.5cqw` and \"PONTETIALE\" to `9.3cqw` improves the overall visual balance and readability while maintaining the text stroke.\n\n\n**Fine-Tuning Vertical Positioning**\n\nI\'m now focusing on vertical alignment, specifically the `bottom` property of `motion.h1` relative to the \"SHOP NOW\" button. I calculated that `bottom: \"-2.2cqw\"` places the text just above the buttons with a sleek gap, considering the button\'s and parent\'s bottom positioning. I also confirmed that `whitespace-nowrap` is essential for both text lines and the heading itself for clean display. I have also verified `left-[0%]` aligns it to the left side.\n\n\n**Verifying Horizontal Alignment**\n\nI\'m now confirming the horizontal alignment between `motion.h1` and the button. Specifically, I\'m checking if the button, positioned at `left: \"38.5%\"` or `left: \"37%\"` aligns with the text, which currently has `left-[0%]` or `left: \"1.5cqw\"`. With `motion.h1` positioned at `37%` of the hero container, the overlap or separation is minimized, maintaining visual harmony. I\'m assessing which positioning is visually cleaner.\n\n\n"
    provider_assigned_message_id: "mmm-aoGhFOXiqtsP8sOV2AE"
  }
